2. The Original Republic Building (Demolished)
A pioneer of the Chicago School of architecture, this building was once a centerpiece of the Loop's retail district. 
Location: Southeast corner of State Street and Adams Street (209 S. State St.).
Architect: Designed by the renowned firm Holabird & Roche.
History: Built between 1902 and 1905, it originally stood 12 stories tall before being raised to 19 stories in 1909. It was one of the first office buildings to utilize concrete caisson foundations extending to bedrock.
Demolition: It was razed in 1961 to make way for the Home Federal Building. Interestingly, the new building saved costs by reusing 44 of the Republic’s original caissons. 
